      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "2015 BMJ Publishing Group Ltd.",
        "AbstractText": "A 43-year-old woman presented to George Washington emergency department with 48\u2005h of new-onset inguinal pain. Physical examination revealed a diffuse maculopapular rash involving the palms and soles, as well as inguinal lymphadenopathy. The patient denied recent travel outside of Washington, DC, and had no known sick contacts. She was admitted to the hospital for observation. Within 24\u2005h of admission she developed left lower extremity flaccid paralysis, with loss of left patellar and Achilles reflexes. cerebrospinal fluid was positive for West Nile virus IgG and IgM antibodies, so methylprednisone 125\u2005mg intravenously two times per day was started. On day 7, the patient recovered reflexes and continued to regain strength in the left lower extremity. She was discharged on day 9 on prednisone taper, with outpatient follow-up."
      },
